# On the Riverbank...
Tamil title: ஆற்றங்கரையிலே...

Written by Anna under the pseudonym "Kaalan."

**Agila:** Why have you returned so soon, Bhuvana?

**Bhuvana:** No, sister. My husband has gone to the neighbouring village for a death ceremony. He may return, and I must go.

**Agila:** What tale are you telling? You hurry as though children were crying at home or your old husband would fondle you the moment he arrived. Sit down; you can go later.

**Bhuvana:** Why abuse my husband? He married me in his declining years so I could cook for him. Must I not attend to him? Who else is there to care for him?

**Agila:** You say I abuse him. You came to him five years ago. Why have you remained without a child? Shall I call you barren?

**Bhuvana:** What can we do about it, sister? God must open his eyes.

**Agila:** You foolish woman! God or brinjal, it all depends on our own cleverness. Look at me. Like you, I became the younger wife of a sixty-five-year-old man. Not even two years have passed, and look at this child, like a bar of gold.

[Her illicit lover Periyanna Chettiar appears in her mind.]

**Bhuvana:** Yes, sister, that is precisely why I am worried. Astrologer Subramania Iyer reportedly told my husband that if we go to Thirumuttam, bathe in the temple tank and circle the peepal tree at the Aswatha Narayana temple, we shall have offspring.

On the next Monday new-moon day, I plan to go to Thirumuttam, circle the tree, and worship Bhuvaraha Swami too. Who knows what he will do?

**Agila:** You simpleton, you are becoming completely mad. Even in this twentieth century, when the flame of reason burns brightly, you believe this.

You fail to understand that this is one of the arrangements our elders made so that they and their descendants might eat comfortably. Even the peepal tree has withered; only the root remains. These Sudra fellows circle it believing a child will come. About seventy Madhwa Brahmins in that town eat comfortably from the offerings given by those wretches who circle the root.

**Bhuvana:** Why speak like this, sister? Thousands of people and wealthy men circle it. Do you say none of them has intelligence?

**Agila:** What doubt is there? Will a child be born merely by circling a withered root? Without circling it, have not millions of Muslims, Christians and Adi Dravidars borne children naturally?

Moreover, our elders carved the names "Ganga," "Narmada," "Durga," "Kaveri," "Yamuna," and all the other rivers of our world upon the steps around the Nitya Pushkarani tank.

The Madhwas gather the men and women who come to circle the root, take them at midnight to the moss-covered part of the tank opposite the word "Ganga," and order them to bathe just as they are.

When they emerge, the priest says in our divine language, Sanskrit, "I offer this sacred bath on the bank of the Ganga." Believing they have acquired the merit of bathing in the Ganga, they pay one rupee and four annas if it is a first marriage, and three rupees eight annas or five rupees for a second or third. They fall at his feet and worship.

Shivering with cold, suffering colds, and some even fever, they then go to circle the withered root.

**Bhuvana:** Is so much hardship required even before circling it? Sister, you always exalt our people, but now you strip away their colour and take away the Sudras' honour as well. What if someone hears you?

**Agila:** I need fear only if I lie. What can anyone do if I state what exists? Listen.

After bathing in wet clothes, they go straight to the temple, give offerings to the Madhwas, and enter the dark enclosure around the root, which is not even three feet wide. Hundreds of couples, men and women, push through the crush. They lose their partner's hand and cannot grasp it again. Shouting and struggling, they forget the prescribed 108 or 1,008 circuits and become exhausted after five or six.

The man searches for the woman and the woman for the man. Desperate merely to escape, each may grasp someone else's hand and rush outside, after which husband and wife reunite.

**Bhuvana:** What is this, sister? Is it so difficult? Are there no policemen there? Will they not make proper arrangements or stop all this?

**Agila:** Did the British not tell us that they do not interfere in religious affairs, and only afterward accept the task of government? How can they interfere in this? You are utterly rustic.

**Bhuvana:** Do even our people go there? If it is so difficult, surely they would not.

**Agila:** That is precisely it. Why would our people circle that root? Are they as ignorant as these Sudras? Even among Sudras there are first-class and second-class people like ours, including many wealthy ones. Such people hear something spoken in our divine language, Sanskrit, assume it is right, give money, fall at the priest's feet, worship, and leave.

If you want more, those Sudras even worship Bhuvaraha Swami.

**Bhuvana:** Sister, you spoke this way about a Madhwa deity. Now you say the same about a Vaishnavite god too.

**Agila:** What else can I do? In Sanskrit, *varaha* means pig in Tamil, and *swami* means master. Why tie a silk tassel to a broom? In Tamil it becomes "pig keeper." If our divine language calls it Bhuvaraha and people think it is a god, give offerings, fall down and worship, who can be angry if they are called fools?

How long will you hide the truth?

**Bhuvana:** Then what those Self-Respect people say is correct. Still, how can we say all this outside?

**Agila:** Even if we do not, look here. My husband and astrologer Subramania Iyer were speaking secretly somewhere. That wretched *Chitra Putran* listened and printed it in the paper without omitting a single letter.

**Bhuvana:** Put everything upon God's head and we shall see later. Come, let us go; it is late. Will you come tomorrow and tell me more about Thirumuttam?

**Agila:** Very well. Come, let us go.

(*Dravida Nadu*, date damaged as 17-66-1945)

---NOTES---
This four-image dialogue is textually continuous and was written under Anna's pseudonym “Kaalan.” The closing date prints the impossible month 66 and remains unresolved. The characters' caste prejudice and derogatory vocabulary are translated as part of the satire, not endorsed or modernised. Historical rupee-anna amounts are preserved.
